# DTF tier manifest: scan-verdict-dbversion  (artifact-keeper#2976)
# Profile-set: filesystem + trivy scanner (parity with the inline-scan-proxy-
# pypi-2954 tier). Grype is the effective CVE engine for filesystem wheels and
# runs from the binary bundled in the runtime image; the tier probes that
# binary's live version string via `docker exec`.
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS (#2976, follow-up LOW from the #2954/#2970 red-team):
#   `proxy_scan_results` verdicts carry `scanner_version` provenance, and
#   `verdict_is_fresh` was BUILT to invalidate a verdict when the live scanner
#   version differs - but the serve-time call site passed `current_version=None`
#   so the comparison never ran. A cached `clean` verdict for a content digest
#   was therefore reused for the full DEDUP_TTL_DAYS (30d) window even after
#   the CVE-DB advanced to newly know a CVE in those bytes: the proxy kept
#   serving `200 X-AK-Scan: clean` from stale cache.
#
# The fix threads the live CVE-engine version (Grype's VersionCache-backed
# `Scanner::version()`, the SAME string persisted as verdict provenance) into
# `verdict_is_fresh` at serve time: a stored/live mismatch marks the verdict
# stale and forces the re-scan branches (fail-closed: inline re-scan -> 403 on
# vulnerable / 423 on inconclusive; never stale-clean).
#
# Discriminating oracle (oracle.sh), fail-closed, injected version delta:
#   A true live CVE-DB bump is impractical in the harness, so the tier injects
#   the delta from the OTHER side: it seeds a `clean` verdict row for a KNOWN-
#   CVE wheel's digest (Jinja2-2.10, flagged by Grype) whose scanner_version is
#   a stale sentinel (`grype-0.0.1-dtf`) that can never equal the live probe.
#   * STALE-VERSION pull of that wheel ->
#       Fixed (#2976):  provenance mismatch -> verdict stale -> inline re-scan
#                       -> 403 scan_blocked, and the digest row is upserted to
#                       `vulnerable` at the LIVE scanner version.
#       Pre-fix (RED):  `current_version=None` -> TTL-only freshness -> the
#                       poisoned stale-clean row serves the CVE wheel
#                       200 X-AK-Scan: clean and the row stays `clean` at the
#                       stale sentinel version.
#   * SAME-VERSION control: a second known-CVE wheel (PyYAML-5.3.1) seeded
#     `clean` AT the live probed version -> 200 X-AK-Scan: clean on BOTH
#     images (the cache is honoured when provenance matches: no needless
#     re-scan, and #2976 is a targeted invalidation, not a cache-off hammer).
#   * PROBE-UNKNOWN leg: threading the version in is not sufficient on its own.
#     `verdict_is_fresh` treats an UNKNOWN version (either side) as "not
#     provably stale", and that fast path runs BEFORE the inline scan, so a
#     cached `clean` verdict short-circuited the whole fail_closed gate whenever
#     the probe failed (engine mid-upgrade: a >=60s window, i.e. exactly during
#     the CVE-DB advance this issue is about; engine absent: permanent; probe
#     past its 5s timeout under load). The tier stubs grype and restarts the
#     backend (dropping the 1h in-process version cache), then asserts:
#       control  a FRESH digest is 423, so the node is provably fail-closed;
#       gate     the cached-clean digest is 423/403, NOT 200 X-AK-Scan: clean.
#     Fail-open is deliberately untouched (its re-scan branch serves anyway).
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: admin login + repo/security setup + several proxy
# pulls back-to-back through one admin.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem scanners.trivy"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
NEEDS_INTERNET="true"
